1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,449
Every day, businesses generate receipts.

2
00:00:04,449 --> 00:00:10,205
And behind every receipt, someone has to turn a piece of paper into an accounting transaction.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,334
Businesses already have their own financial systems.

4
00:00:16,334 --> 00:00:18,202
We didn't want to replace them.

5
00:00:18,202 --> 00:00:21,396
We wanted to automate the work happening inside them.

6
00:00:21,396 --> 00:00:22,783
Introducing LedgerFlow.

7
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,469
Connect your existing Excel workbook.

8
00:00:28,469 --> 00:00:35,337
LedgerFlow identifies its accounts, its structure, and the exact places where new entries can safely be written.

9
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,792
Employees don't need to understand double-entry bookkeeping.

10
00:00:42,792 --> 00:00:46,085
They capture a receipt, and send it to their workspace.

11
00:00:46,085 --> 00:00:47,462
The iPhone is optional.

12
00:00:47,462 --> 00:00:49,019
A direct upload works too.

13
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:57,385
One receipt, one workflow, with the browser-based agent at its core.

14
00:00:57,385 --> 00:01:00,400
Seconds later, the receipt is waiting on the Mac.

15
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:08,490
Vultr Serverless Inference reads the receipt and turns it into structured financial information.

16
00:01:08,490 --> 00:01:12,678
Every value is linked to the text it came from, and still has to be checked.

17
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:19,619
Financial decisions cannot depend on model confidence alone.

18
00:01:19,619 --> 00:01:26,058
Deterministic accounting rules build a balanced entry, and define the exact cells the agent is allowed to change.

19
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:32,776
LedgerFlow doesn't just recommend an entry.

20
00:01:32,776 --> 00:01:36,311
It uses Vultr to generate a program that performs the work.

21
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:44,988
Every generated program runs in a disposable sandbox.

22
00:01:44,988 --> 00:01:50,705
No network, no secrets, one CPU, five hundred twelve megabytes, a hard deadline.

23
00:01:50,705 --> 00:01:52,992
Then the container is destroyed.

24
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:58,195
But what happens when the code misbehaves?

25
00:01:58,195 --> 00:02:04,002
Here, a hostile program tries the network, tries to overwrite its input, and then never stops.

26
00:02:04,002 --> 00:02:09,316
The sandbox blocks each attempt, kills it at the deadline, and destroys the container.

27
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:21,563
Running is not enough.

28
00:02:21,563 --> 00:02:30,610
An independent verifier opens the resulting workbook, checks every change, keeps formulas and features intact, and confirms the entry is balanced.

29
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:36,903
The accountant sees exactly what will change.

30
00:02:36,903 --> 00:02:41,768
Approval is bound to the verified file itself, not to the model's recommendation.

31
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:47,257
The result is a real Excel workbook.

32
00:02:47,257 --> 00:02:51,714
Eight cells written, nothing else touched, and the original version preserved.

33
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:06,804
We compared eighteen model configurations on real Vultr inference, attacked the sandbox with hostile programs, and measured how well existing workbooks are preserved.

34
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:18,272
Cloud inference, deterministic controls, sandboxed execution, and independent verification.

35
00:03:18,272 --> 00:03:22,810
An agent that doesn't just answer questions, but performs real work.

36
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,553
Your employees capture the receipts.

37
00:03:28,553 --> 00:03:30,449
LedgerFlow performs the work.

38
00:03:30,449 --> 00:03:31,167
LedgerFlow.

39
00:03:31,167 --> 00:03:33,717
Every receipt lands in the right cells.

40
00:03:33,717 --> 00:03:34,436
With proof.

